# What may NOT enter a `docker build .` context.
#
# Docker's build context is the FILESYSTEM, not `git ls-files` --
# `.gitignore` has no effect here at all. Without this file, `Dockerfile`'s
# `COPY . .` copies whatever is on disk on the box doing the build: `.env`
# (mandatory for `web` to start, so it exists on every working install,
# and it carries SECRET_KEY and DATABASE_URL), the whole managed document
# store and backups under `data/`, and the entire `.git` history. An image
# layer is immutable -- deleting a file afterwards does not unbake it, and
# `docker history` reads a layer back with no running container and no
# credentials (security audit S3, dependency audit IMAGE-6).
#
# NOT a copy of `.gitignore`, deliberately. The two answer different
# questions -- "may this be committed" vs. "may this enter an image layer"
# -- and only their intersection looks the same; `.env` is the clearest
# divergence. `foundation/ops/tests/test_repo_hygiene.py` pins the
# patterns below that a future edit may not silently drop.

# --- Secrets & local config ---
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets/
*.key
*.pem

# --- Operator data (the managed document store, backups, the DB dir) ---
data/

# --- Local build/venv artefacts (a host .venv is also the wrong
#     architecture inside the image, so copying it is worse than useless) ---
.venv/
venv/
env/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# --- Version control and agent/session working directories ---
.git/
.github/
.claude/
.superpowers/

# --- Documentation the running image never reads ---
# `**/*.md`, not `*.md`: a .dockerignore pattern is matched against the
# whole relative path with Go `filepath.Match` semantics, where `*` does
# not cross a path separator, so `*.md` alone would exclude root-level
# markdown only and leave every docs/*.md and app README.md in the image.
# `Dockerfile` never COPYs a `.md` file explicitly, so none is kept back
# in with a negation -- a container shell has no front page, and that is
# fine, because nothing at runtime reads one (foundation/ops/backup.py's
# references are in docstrings and tests, not code paths).
docs/superpowers/
**/*.md

# --- OS noise ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
